      <!-- ── SECTION 2 ── -->
      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>PTFE Aerosol Lubricant Mechanisms</h2>
        <p>Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) remains one of the most broadly specified active ingredients in light-duty industrial applications due to its exceptionally low coefficient of friction. When dispensed as a PTFE aerosol lubricant, the microscopic fluoropolymer particles rapidly bond to the metallic or polymer substrate, effectively filling in microscopic surface asperities. According to data available through <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Polytetrafluoroethylene" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem</a>, PTFE's highly inert molecular structure allows it to establish a remarkably smooth, non-stick dry film layer that repels moisture, resists most harsh chemicals, and prevents galling during sliding friction. This makes it an essential choice for cleanroom environments, textile machinery, and intricate mechanisms where traditional <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/chain-lubricant-aerosols" target="_top">chain lubricant aerosols</a> would cause unacceptable contamination.</p>
