      <!-- ── SECTION 3 ── -->
      <section id="section-3">
        <h2>MoS2 Spray Lubricant for Extreme Pressures</h2>
        <p>Molybdenum disulfide (MoS2) represents a fundamental departure from fluoropolymers, leveraging a unique crystalline architecture composed of molybdenum atoms sandwiched between layers of sulfur atoms. An MoS2 spray lubricant relies on this lamellar structure to deliver exceptional boundary lubrication; the inherently weak van der Waals forces between the sulfur planes allow them to slide past one another with minimal mechanical resistance. Standardized wear tests, such as those governed by <a href="https://www.astm.org/d4172-21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM D4172</a> for lubricating fluids, demonstrate that MoS2 excels in extreme pressure (EP) applications where heavy loads would otherwise displace a conventional liquid oil or easily strip away a softer PTFE dry film.</p>

        <!-- GREEN CALLOUT -->
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Insight</strong>
          Particle size engineering is the most critical factor in MoS2 spray lubricants. If the dispersed particles exceed the optimal micron range, they risk clogging the aerosol valve; if they are too small, they may agglomerate within the suspension, severely compromising the uniformity of the boundary lubrication under extreme mechanical loads.
        </div>
      </section>

